{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "one",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-07-12",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  // ⚠️ NO `routes` HERE, DELIBERATELY. Declaring them makes `wrangler dev`
  // rewrite the incoming Host to the route's hostname, which collapses every
  // door onto one — and the doors ARE the tenancy (see `kernel/door.ts`), so a
  // collapsed Host is a development environment where none of this works and
  // nothing says why. The production routes are a dashboard step.

  // ⚠️ THIS BLOCK IS THE DEPLOYED CONFIG. Local overrides go in `.dev.vars`
  // (copy `.dev.vars.example`), which wrangler reads in preference to this — so
  // a value here is what a real request sees, and nothing else.
  "vars": {
    // ⚠️ `ROOT` IS WHAT `doorFor` MEASURES EVERY HOSTNAME AGAINST, so a wrong
    // one is not a misconfiguration — it is a deployment where every door is
    // `none` and every request 404s. It read `localhost` here for exactly as
    // long as this file was never deployed.
    //
    // ⚠️ AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT `4dl.app`. Kova's production routes are
    // `kova.4dl.app/*` and `*.kova.4dl.app/*`; putting One at the apex would
    // make `*.4dl.app/*` One's, and One's wildcard would then match a live
    // tenant's address. One label of its own keeps the two disjoint, which is
    // the whole isolation while Kova has not migrated.
    //
    // `t` for tenant: every door is one label under it, so a workspace is
    // `<slug>.t.4dl.app` — shorter to say, shorter to type, and shorter on the
    // wire in every request a screen or a phone makes.
    "ROOT": "t.4dl.app",

    // ⚠️ NEVER `development` HERE. The development lane puts sign-in codes in
    // retained logs and turns on the lane that fabricates AI output and bills
    // for it. `.dev.vars` is what turns it on locally.
    "ENVIRONMENT": "production"
  },

  "dev": {
    "port": 8080,
    // The whole point of the local port: every door is a subdomain of it.
    "local_protocol": "http"
  },

  "assets": {
    // ⚠️ A FILESYSTEM PATH, NOT A PACKAGE DEPENDENCY — which is why `turbo.json`
    // declares the build edge by hand. Nothing in the dependency graph connects
    // this worker to OneSpace's build, and a missing one does not fail: the
    // worker boots and serves nothing, which reads as a broken deploy rather
    // than a missing step.
    "directory": "../one-space/dist",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    // The SPA owns its routing; every unknown path is its business.
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application"
  },

  // ⚠️ MAIL LEAVES THROUGH THIS AND NOWHERE ELSE. `destination_address` is what
  // Cloudflare requires on a `send_email` binding that is not restricted to one
  // address; the SENDER is `email.from` in the operator console, because that is
  // the value somebody rotates. A deployment without this binding refuses to send
  // rather than answering "check your email" over nothing — see `mail.ts`.
  //
  // ⚠️ AND THE ZONE HAS TO HAVE THE SENDER VERIFIED. That is a dashboard step per
  // zone and there is no way around it: a plausible-looking address that bounces
  // is worse than a refusal, because the refusal is visible.
  "send_email": [{ "name": "SEND_EMAIL" }],

  // ⚠️ BOUND FOR THE GATEWAY'S LOG, NOT TO RUN A MODEL. Every generation goes
  // out over HTTP to AI Gateway's `/compat` endpoint — one shape for every
  // provider, Cloudflare's own models included — and this binding exists so the
  // cost of each call can be read back out of the gateway afterwards. That
  // figure is the only number in the metering chain that is not our own
  // arithmetic, which is what makes it worth a binding.
  //
  // ⚠️ IT COSTS NOTHING UNBOUND-BUT-UNUSED. No call is made through it unless a
  // gateway NAME is configured, so a deployment that has not been told about a
  // gateway simply never checks a margin — and the console says so rather than
  // showing one nobody is measuring.
  "ai": { "binding": "AI" },

  // ⚠️ THE SEARCH NAMESPACE IS DELIBERATELY NOT BOUND HERE, AND THE REASON IS AN
  // OUTAGE THIS FILE CAUSED. `ai_search_namespaces` was added, and wrangler
  // RESOLVES that binding against the account during `deploy` — so a token
  // without the AI Search permission failed the whole deploy, not the feature:
  //
  //     ✘ [ERROR] A request to the Cloudflare API
  //       (/accounts/…/ai-search/namespaces/default) failed.
  //       Authentication error [code: 10000]
  //
  // Four commits shipped nothing while every other capability was fine. A
  // binding hand-written here is resolved at deploy time by definition, so it
  // can only ever fail in the one place a failure costs the most.
  //
  // ⚠️ AND THE FEATURE DEGRADES HONESTLY WITHOUT IT, which is what makes leaving
  // it out the right call rather than a retreat. The index JOB needs no binding
  // — it holds the account token and uses the items API — so records are still
  // marked and carried. Only the QUERY needs this, and with no `AI_SEARCH` in
  // the env `ctx.find` is absent and the generated find operation says search is
  // not on here. See `serve.ts`.
  //
  // ⚠️ AND `--dry-run` DOES NOT CATCH IT — measured. It lists the binding and
  // exits without ever calling the account, so the one check anybody would
  // reach for passes while the real deploy fails. There is no local test for
  // this; the deploy IS the test.
  //
  // To turn querying on: give the deploy token AI Search read, then restore
  // `"ai_search_namespaces": [{ "binding": "AI_SEARCH", "namespace": "default" }]`
  // in a commit of its OWN — so if the token is still wrong, the only thing that
  // does not ship is the binding.

  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DIRECTORY",
      "database_name": "one-directory",
      // ⚠️ A PLACEHOLDER UNTIL PROVISIONING WRITES THE REAL ONE. A deploy with
      // this id in place would bind a database that does not exist and answer
      // every request 500 — so nothing ships until it is replaced.
      "database_id": "2e42d7a5-7c5d-4300-aacd-d09246ba7130"
    },
    {
      "binding": "SHARD_EU_1",
      "database_name": "one-shard-eu-1",
      "database_id": "afe4e35c-bf44-48ff-b43e-c55b913efcda"
    }
  ],

  // ⚠️ THE CLOCK TICKS HOURLY AND DECIDES NOTHING. It used to be `0 3 * * *`,
  // and while one pass ran everything that was the same statement twice: the
  // trigger WAS the schedule, so every job's declared cron was decoration and
  // 03:00 was the only cadence this deployment could express.
  //
  // ⚠️ SO THE TRIGGER IS A HEARTBEAT AND THE SCHEDULE IS THE JOB'S. Each pass
  // asks every job whether it is due (`dueNow`) and runs the ones that are, so
  // a job declared hourly is hourly and one declared `0 3 * * *` still fires
  // once, at 03:00. Twenty-three of the twenty-four passes are a handful of
  // indexed reads that answer no.
  //
  // ⚠️ AND AN HOURLY HEARTBEAT IS WHAT MAKES RESCHEDULING REAL. An operator can
  // move a job from the console without a deploy; against a daily trigger,
  // moving one to anything finer than a day would have been a setting that
  // saved successfully and changed nothing.
  //
  // ⚠️ A MISSED TICK IS NOT A MISSED RUN, which is the other half. Due means
  // "should have fired by now" rather than "is firing this minute", so a cold
  // start or a skipped hour runs the job late instead of dropping it in silence.
  //
  // ⚠️ AND A HANDLER WITHOUT THIS DECLARATION NEVER RUNS. `scheduled` in the
  // worker compiles, typechecks, is unit-tested and is called by nothing — the
  // exact shape this deployment is a catalogue of, one config file over.
  "triggers": { "crons": ["0 * * * *"] },

  "observability": { "enabled": true }
}
